+# Runway internal runner pilot
+
+## Purpose
+
+Central stores the bounded runner pool and lease lifecycle. The Runway process owns GitHub registration, event polling, and guest transport. Atlas calls remain inside `central/integrations/runway.py`, through the existing Team-authorized client. This pilot has no billing or customer dashboard.
+
+## Authorization and ownership
+
+Pool configuration is operator-only. A service user has `Central User`, a team-wide role granting `server:view`, `server:create`, and `server:terminate`, and must equal the pool's `controller_user`. `System Manager` remains the only operator bypass. Both DocType list and document permission hooks enforce team-wide read access. Direct customer lifecycle writes are denied.
+
+A controller claims a pool with a unique `owner_id`. Every successful claim increments the fence, including a same-owner reclaim. Another owner waits for expiry. Ownership lasts 60 seconds; the controller renews before expiry. Every lease RPC checks identity, fence, and expiry under the pool row lock.
+
+## Admission and lifecycle
+
+`Runner Pool` stores Team, region, repository, scale set, image, resources, SSH public key, concurrency, and deadline policy. Pool identity and image cannot change while any lease has unfinished VM or GitHub cleanup. `Runner Lease` is an immutable-identity audit record with its attempt UUID, runner identity, observed VM, job identity, deadlines, result, and error.
+
+Reservation locks the Team and pool before counting active leases. The pool limit applies alongside a fixed internal-pilot maximum of four leases per Team. A lease continues to consume capacity until phase is `deleted` and `jit_state` is `removed`. Snapshot conflicts retry only rolled-back database admission using the same lease UUID; this never retries a remote mutation.
+
+`max_job_seconds` currently means total lease lifetime from reservation, including provisioning and bootstrap. It is not execution-only billing time. Guest runtime limits must be compatible with this configured deadline.
+
+Phases are `reserved`, `provisioning`, `uncertain`, `vm_ready`, `jit_pending`, `jit_ready`, `running`, `deleting`, and `deleted`. JIT material is never stored here. The controller persists `jit_pending` before registration; an uncertain registration is retired rather than repeated. GitHub removal is separately recorded with `runner_removed`.
+
+## Regional reconciliation
+
+Creation intent is committed before one Atlas POST. A lost response retains the attempt and searches pool/lease tags; it never resends creation. If no matching VM can be found, the uncertain lease remains capacity-consuming, even after expiry. Operator investigation is required to establish an authoritative outcome. Tags do not provide atomic idempotency.
+
+The scheduler checks leases every ten seconds, expires them into cleanup, and confirms VM absence with a scoped GET. An accepted DELETE is not completion. GitHub runner cleanup remains the controller's responsibility, including after scheduler-driven VM deletion.
+
+An orphan scan selects only the current pool's tenant-scoped VMs with matching `runway_pool`, `runway_lease`, `team`, and CI role tags. It requests deletion only when no corresponding lease exists. Network failures leave visible pool or lease errors. Configure the bench scheduler tick to ten seconds and run its worker queues; merely installing DocTypes does not run reconciliation.
+
+## RPC contract
+
+Authenticated POST calls use `/api/method/central.api.runway.<method>` and Frappe's `message` envelope. Calls are `claim_pool`, `renew_pool`, `reserve_lease`, `update_lease`, `ensure_vm`, and `request_delete`. Except claim, each carries `pool`, `owner_id`, and `fence`; lease calls also carry `lease_id`.
+
+Claim and renew return the fence, pool configuration, and leases whose cleanup is incomplete. Times are ISO UTC. A reservation uses a caller-generated UUID and returns the same lease when repeated. VM provisioning and cleanup return the latest lease snapshot.
+
+Update events are `acquire_requested`, `acquired`, `jit_requested`, `jit_ready`, `bootstrapped`, `started`, `completed`, `failed`, and `runner_removed`. Acquisition identity is persisted before GitHub acquisition. Positive request IDs and job/runner identities cannot be replaced within a lease. A zero-request direct assignment uses the job UUID and expected runner in Runway.
+
+## Validation
+
+`central.tests.test_runway` exercises real concurrent reservations, duplicate capacity, fencing, authorization denial, cross-team reads, lifecycle write denial, uncertain creation and discovery, expired VM deletion, independent GitHub cleanup, owned orphan cleanup, and preservation of cleanup intent during uncertain responses. Run it only on a separate test site.
+
+### Job evidence
+
+Runner Lease records repository, request labels, run attempt (when known), job display name, job workflow ref and event name from available, assigned, started and completed events. The assigned event enriches identity without advancing the lease phase. The started snapshot flag is set only by a started event. A direct assignment can have request ID zero and reveal job identity only after VM creation and runner registration. Missing evidence preserves earlier observations; conflicting nonempty runner, job, workflow, repository, labels or positive request IDs are refused. Started events require a running lease; the controller first resolves an interrupted bootstrap acknowledgment when needed.
+
+This recording contract does not establish repository/image compatibility before VM creation. The controller must continue provisioning from demand even when job identity has not arrived.
+
+## Regional cache
+
+Runner Pool `regional_cache` defaults off. The pool sweep reconciles one marked
+Team Service bucket per Team/region, named `<tenant>-<region>-runway-cache`.
+It drains and deletes it after the last Active flagged pool disappears. Team
+locking serializes reconciles; ordinary buckets are never adopted or drained.
+Use existing storage usage/quota/rotation and billing machinery. Lifecycle is
+eventual, outside document-save transactions, to avoid reversing lease locks.
+
+Region `cache_service_url` is HTTPS. Operator `enroll_cache` POSTs `/configure`
+using `Authorization: Bearer <jwt>`, with issuer `central`, audience
+`runway-cache:<numeric region id>`, scope `runway-cache-configure`, and the
+generic regional signer's five-minute TTL. Verify using Central's issuer URL/JWKS
+from `central.sso.central_url()` / `jwks_url()`. The cache service never accepts
+or holds Cargo-audience tokens. JSON has exactly `central_url`, `region`, `credential`; success is HTTP
+200 with exactly `{"configured": true, "ca": "<public CA PEM>"}`.
+Region stores `cache_service_ca` after checking a single CA certificate and its
+DNS SAN against configured `cache_service_name` (`internal-name:guest-port`).
+Re-enrolment replaces the public CA and rotates the read credential together.
+Only pools with `regional_cache` expose `regional_cache: true`, `cache_service`
+and `cache_ca` in the pool snapshot; credentials never appear there.
+The service must implement this contract; no Cargo modifications are required.
+
+`Authorization: token <credential>` permits only GET `central.runway.cache_lease`
+(address) and `central.runway.cache_bucket` (team). Raw JSON has no Frappe wrapper.
+Lease keys: lease, team, tenant_id, region, repository, vm_id, mesh_address,
+runner_id, runner_name, job, deadline. Job is null before assignment/start;
+otherwise keys are job_id, request_id, run_id, run_attempt, display_name,
+workflow_ref, event_name, started, ref, sha, verified. Attempt zero is unverified.
+The lease also includes repository_id and default_branch, verified by the controller.
+Only verified jobs may receive grants; the service holds no GitHub credential.
+Bucket keys: team, tenant_id, bucket, endpoint, region, access_key, secret_key.
+Reads are region-scoped; 404 means no result, 409 means ambiguous lease.
+Errors have exactly `error` (`not_found` or `ambiguous`).
